{"cluster_id":"cl-001","content_sha256":"09e15657275a316f2947d35fc4bb590ccd1d407c7eaf27c9dc58b218d10ab4fe","created_at":"2026-08-12T22:35:36.166615+00:00","independence_groups":["group-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6"],"model_role":"semantic-router","parent_artifacts":["03_clusters/enriched_units.jsonl"],"prompt_version":"pass-02b-cluster-labeling-v3.0","related_existing_topics":["fairy tales and folklore","the soul in nineteenth-century literature","bargains and their conditions","Hans Christian Andersen"],"routing_confidence":0.8,"routing_reason":"One story, and every unit is either a rule of its world, a term of the bargain, or a step in the causal chain that connects the two. They belong in one comparison set because the units are not independent facts about a subject -- they are clauses of a single system that only makes sense together. The three-hundred-year lifespan explains why she wants a soul; the grandmother's conditions explain why she needs the prince; the witch's terms explain what she pays and what happens if she fails; and the daughters of the air at the end revise the first rule. Splitting the setting from the bargain would leave a reader with terms and no stakes.","run_id":"mermaid","schema_version":"3.1.0","topic_label":"the mermaid's bargain for an immortal soul, and the rules that govern it","unit_ids":["u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0001","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0002","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0003","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0004","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0005","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0006","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0007","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0008","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0009","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0010","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0011","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0012","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0013","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0014","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0015","u-src-the-little-mermaid-df5d02e6-0016